3A — Commands Used
1. Find latest Amazon Linux 2023 AMI
aws ec2 describe-images \
  --owners amazon \
  --filters "Name=name,Values=al2023-ami-*-x86_64" \
  --query "sort_by(Images, &CreationDate)[-1].ImageId" \
  --output text

Save it:

AMI_ID=ami-xxxxxxxx
2. Check existing key pairs
aws ec2 describe-key-pairs \
  --query "KeyPairs[*].KeyName" \
  --output table

If you need to create one:

aws ec2 create-key-pair \
  --key-name assignment-key \
  --query "KeyMaterial" \
  --output text > assignment-key.pem

chmod 400 assignment-key.pem
KEY_NAME=assignment-key
3. Launch EC2
aws ec2 run-instances \
  --image-id $AMI_ID \
  --instance-type t2.micro \
  --subnet-id $PUBLIC_A_ID \
  --security-group-ids $SG_ID \
  --key-name $KEY_NAME \
  --associate-public-ip-address

Save instance ID:

INSTANCE_ID=i-xxxxxxxx

Wait:

aws ec2 wait instance-running \
  --instance-ids $INSTANCE_ID

Check details:

aws ec2 describe-instances \
  --instance-ids $INSTANCE_ID \
  --query "Reservations[0].Instances[0].[InstanceId,State.Name,PrivateIpAddress,PublicIpAddress]" \
  --output table
4. SSH into EC2
ssh -i assignment-key.pem ec2-user@$PUBLIC_IP

Now you're inside the EC2 instance.

⭐ 5. IMDS — Most Important Part

IMDS = Instance Metadata Service

Its endpoint is:

169.254.169.254

This is a special link-local IP available from inside the EC2 instance.

Think:

EC2
 │
 │ HTTP request
 ▼
169.254.169.254
 │
 ▼
IMDS
6. Get IMDSv2 token

Amazon Linux 2023 normally uses IMDSv2.

TOKEN=$(curl -X PUT \
  http://169.254.169.254/latest/api/token \
  -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token-ttl-seconds: 21600")

The token is then used to access metadata.

7. Get instance ID from IMDS
curl \
  -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token: $TOKEN" \
  http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/instance-id

You'll get something like:

i-0123456789abcdef

This is interesting because you didn't ask AWS API.

You asked the local EC2 metadata service.

8. Get IAM information
curl \
  -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token: $TOKEN" \
  http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/info

If an IAM role is attached, you'll get information about the role/instance profile.

⭐⭐⭐ 9. The MOST Important IMDS Command

To see the IAM role associated with the instance:

curl \
  -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token: $TOKEN" \
  http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/

Suppose it returns:

EC2S3Role

Then:

curl \
  -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token: $TOKEN" \
  http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/EC2S3Role

You can get something conceptually like:

{
  "AccessKeyId": "ASIA...",
  "SecretAccessKey": "...",
  "Token": "...",
  "Expiration": "..."
}

These are temporary credentials.

🔥 Understand IMDS Properly

This is the part I really want you to remember:

                IAM
                 │
                 ▼
          IAM Role + Policy
                 │
                 ▼
        Temporary credentials
                 │
                 ▼
              IMDS
       169.254.169.254
